{
  "id": "proofs.divisibility.square-mod-3",
  "course": "proofs",
  "topic": "divisibility",
  "kind": "divisibility_proof",
  "title": "Squares of numbers ≡ 1 (mod 3)",
  "prompt": "Prove: if $n \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$, then $n^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$.",
  "answer_format": "A structured proof: let, case, expr and witness steps (notes optional). No final answer box.",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "tags": [
    "direct proof",
    "congruence"
  ],
  "data": {
    "variables": [
      "n"
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      {
        "var": "n",
        "divisor": 3,
        "remainder": 1
      }
    ],
    "target": "n^2",
    "goal": {
      "divisor": 3,
      "remainder": 1
    }
  },
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "n ≡ 1 (mod 3) means n leaves remainder 1 when divided by 3. Write n that way with a new letter.",
    "Your goal is to write n² as 3·(an integer) + 1."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "Since $n \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$, $n = 3k + 1$ for some integer $k$.",
    "Then $n^2 = 9k^2 + 6k + 1 = 3(3k^2 + 2k) + 1$, and $3k^2 + 2k$ is an integer, so $n^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod 3$. $\\blacksquare$"
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "sundstrom-mr3",
    "section": "3.5 The Division Algorithm and Congruence",
    "note": "Original problem."
  },
  "review": {
    "status": "retired",
    "notes": "Invented problem, retired 2026-11-06: MATH 123 proofs now map 1:1 to Book of Proof exercises."
  },
  "examples": [
    {
      "name": "direct proof",
      "submission": {
        "steps": [
          {
            "kind": "let",
            "content": "n = 3k + 1"
          },
          {
            "kind": "expr",
            "content": "(3k+1)^2"
          },
          {
            "kind": "expr",
            "content": "9k^2 + 6k + 1"
          },
          {
            "kind": "expr",
            "content": "3(3k^2 + 2k) + 1"
          },
          {
            "kind": "witness",
